r the  betterment of other people  so it would be a question of how do we  think about this i think part of it is  just being mindful of how privilege  exercises itself  so if you can walk into certain spaces  around town and get treated better than  other people then you need to bring  other people along and point that out to  make sure that people get treated  equitably it could even be um you know  in simple stuff like you know especially  for somebody like me who's coming from a  minority group who's not always used to  being in positions of power you know  i've been in positions where  people have created  black organizations in an attempt to try  to integrate systems  and  when they get into positions of power to  do the exact same things as people did  who were oppressing them before and i  was like we weren't trying to break into  the old boys club to be the old boys we  were trying to smash this thing up so  remember that you're supposed to smash  the thing up like what those folks were  doing was wrong you're not trying to  join in their madness you're supposed to  change it  so you've got to kind of keep that in  mind and if it involves having people be  accountable to one another for you to  get called on the fact that you're  acting the same way that everybody else  did and it wasn't right when they did it  and it's no more right when you do it  then so be it i think it's also just  being mindful of the little ways in  which structure matters and in which  structure is not always pointed out as a  plausible explanation for why certain  things are wrong so that then we can  consider fixing it so you know i just  just wanted to ask the show of hounds  how many of you heard about the study  that came out of princeton it was widely  publicized in the past couple of days  about how mortality rates among 25 to 50  year old whites are increasing you heard  this story so okay  and it was actually really interesting  to watch the sort of narrative and the  report about the study oh my gosh white  people are dying more it's like black  people and latino people and native  american people been dying for years and  nobody seemed to care  so the fact that we're all up in arms  because white people are dying it's not  i don't want white people to die i just  want to be very  not trying to kill off white people but  the fact that we seem to be more alarmed  about this than we have about high  mortality rates in minority communities  is troubling and it was funny i was  watching the news last night listening  to a story where people were trying to  explain this higher incidence of  mortality has a lot to do with substance  abuse and it has a lot to do with  structural issues like people lost their  jobs  and they might be self-medicating  through this you know drug and alcohol  use so i was like wow all of a sudden  this is a structural issue to deal with  white mortality but when we point this  stuff out in black communities it's  black people's fault i was like well  there's something wrong with this  narrative i'm sure the people were  having this discussion were well-meaning  but this was a messed up conversation  where systematic systemic stuff is okay  when we're talking about white people  but it's not okay when we're talking  about black people we're gonna have to  call people out on that and sort of call  people on their hypocrisy about that and  not practice that type of hypocrisy and  then i just think on a personal level we  all have biases  and so sometimes we aren't aware of them  but sometimes they manifest themselves  through examples it could be through art  it could be through literature or it  could be through personal conversations  i think we have to be receptive to  people pointing out those biases so that  we don't act on them in a way that  actually hurts other people and gives us  unfair advantage compared to others  this is the last question to you both  james  where do you see the hope  what do you see 