n  and this is not peculiar to new haven no  but it's bad here all over the country  yeah that's right that's right  i want to i want to raise this last  question although i think we've covered  so much  really  um  you know  when you see  so much of these  so many of these problems it's so easy  to see them as intractable  you know  i mean  as you say  you go by that construction site  i've been by then i've seen the same  thing oh yeah  i've seen the same thing your department  is right up the block right  and i walk around yale's campus and  there's a sense in which a lot of black  people see this as a white space as it  were  see  and so all these issues you can feel  hopeless you can feel that  this problem is intractable  and what do you do with that what do you  do with that  how do you how do you keep on keeping on  when you see that again and again all  over the place  you see  it's almost a rhetorical question i  think we've kind of spoken to this  really  so i think maybe we should begin to take  uh yeah i think  if you want to take that take it but if  you don't we can we can move on and uh  yeah no i'm ready to turn it over to i  think well first of all this was an  absolutely extraordinary conversation  so i recognize that it is now  five after nine and  some of you may need to be leaving  i'm going to take the liberty to take  about about 10 extra minutes to ask some  questions  but if you need to leave please please  please don't leave without filling out  your card  and  the rest of you can stay put for maybe  about 10 more minutes while we ask our  speakers a couple more questions  um so to both very quickly at yale in  the last few days many uh black students  have said they are exhausted with  educating white students about their  experience  will that ever change  no  probably not you just i mean yeah there  are certain things i i what i would love  to tell black students is you don't have  to bear the burden of being the  representative of all 